KCLim wrote:

> Hello all,
> 
> Below is the summary error log obtained from a VAX 4000-600. A few
> questions to ask and hopefully you guys expert in this sort of analysis
> can help me.
> 
> 1. What is the device PKA0: and UCB?

PKA0 is the SCSI controller.

UCB is "Unit Control Block", and is the data structure in memory that VMS 
uses to describe the device in question - in the information below, the 
devices are PKA0 and MKA500.

The UCB errors refers to errors reported by the device, vs. a timeout 
error.  When VMS initiates an I/O, it starts a timer for a reasonable 
length of time.  A timeout error occurs when the timer expires before the 
I/O completes.  

> 2. What differentiates between a hard error and a soft?

Soft: I got an error, but when I retried the command it worked.

Hard: I got an error, and after one or more retries (depending on the error 
and the I/O operation), I still got an error.  So, I gave up and returned 
an error code to the user program.  This is not necessarily bad.  For 
example, if you INIT a brand new tape, the INIT command will attempt to 
read the volume label on the tape.  The tape doesn't have anything written 
on it, so the tape drive reports a hard error.  However, INIT is smart 
enough to realize that it's trying to read a blank tape, and just ignores 
the error, and proceeds to write the tape.  Likewise if you're using BACKUP 
to init brand new tapes.

> 3. For tape drive MKA500:, is there a threshold of hard error before I
> should get really concerned??

As with most open ended questions, "it depends".

What are the errors?  What was happening at the time of the error?  Etc., 
etc...

        Stu


