          "KCLim" <karchoon@tm.net.my> wrote:

> Hello all,
> 
> Below is the summary error log obtained from a VAX 4000-600. A few questions
> to ask and hopefully you guys expert in this sort of analysis can help me.
> 
> 1. What is the device PKA0: and UCB?

PKA0 is the SCSI controller. The UCB is the Unit Control block. I am not clear why the UCB errors differ from the total of hard and soft errors.

> 2. What differentiates between a hard error and a soft?

Can't give a good enough answer

> 3. For tape drive MKA500:, is there a threshold of hard error before I
> should get really concerned??

In this case I would not worry about MKA500 at first.
See below

> 
> Any help is greatly appreciated.
> 
> Regards
> KC Lim
> ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
> -------
> 
> 
> DEVICE SUMMARY LOGGED BY SID 13000202
> 
>                  ERRORS          TIMEOUTS       UCB ERROR   UCB OPERATION
>              [HARD]  [SOFT]   [HARD]  [SOFT]      COUNT         COUNT
> 
>  _MALA$PKA0:
>    ""
>                 63.      0.       0.      0.           67.            8.
>             ------- -------  ------- -------  ------------ -------------
>     TOTALS      63.      0.       0.      0.           67.            8.
> 
>  _MALA$MKA500:
>    ""
>                 35.      1.       0.      0.           36.        14031.
>             ------- -------  ------- -------  ------------ -------------
>     TOTALS      35.      1.       0.      0.           36.        14031.
> ANALYZE/ERROR_LOG/SINCE=1-SEP-2002
> 00:00:00.00/OUTPUT=SYS$ERRORLOG:ERRLOG.LIS/SUMMARY=(DEVICE,MEMORY)/NOFULL

The SCSI controller is logging more errors than the tape drive. This to me
indicates that the problem is with the SCSI bus, not the tape drive.
Possible reasons:

Bus incorrectly terminated.
SCSI interface in tape drive (not the drive itself) is giving trouble.

We had a TLZ7L which did this a lot. (Every 2-3 months). Power cycling the
drive made it go away in many cases. Replacing the drive and/or power supply
was needed in the other cases. The underlying problem seemed to be that the
PSU design was marginal for the electrical load.

The effect to VMS was that the drive would hang, or more often, report
"offline" or "parity error" during operations. (Incidentally in those cases
error counts would be 1000 - 10000 overnight.)

