No need to assume. It's fact.  See the VMS FAQ:

  
<http://www.openvms.compaq.com/wizard/faq/vmsfaq_009.html#index_x_389>

> First supposition: OS uses firmware to deal with disk, not its' native
> 32Bit drivers. In this case - yes, I just not reached 1.07Gb limit and
> when I reach it I will have disaster at home.

The problem is in the firmware. Take your 4.5 GB disk. A standard
INITialize command will place the file system 'root' files at 2.25 GB.
This placement mininizes the seek distance to these files. The
3100's firmware cannot address these files.

> 
> Second supposition is that VMS does not uses firmware to drive the disk.
> Instead it uses its' own 32-bit driver so limitation is about 8G*<sector
> size

>  And I suppose that this one is right because I don't see the
> difference between system and data disk from OSs' point of view.

Correct.

> In this case we only should place main OS files (kernel, drivers, etc)
> to first 1.07 Gb of disk.

No. The first time you do a backup/image and restore to 'defrag' the
system disk the file system will be re-centered about 2.25 GB. Your
system disk is now not bootable.

