We need to be clear whether it's a _MicroVax_ or a _VAXstation_, as
the back panels are _very_ different. I think Tom is talking about the
_VAXStation_. I'll tell you about the _MicroVax_.

> > First of all, where can I get FAQs, Manuals in Inet for this model?
> 
> Go to Google, type in "Micro Vax Manual".  It's a good start.
> 
> > Second - I see "centronics"-like connector on the back side of machine.
> > Is it ordinary SCSI bus? Why does it not terminated and everything works OK?
> 
> It is a SCSI port. Does not need to be terminated. Someone here will know if
> it's SCSI 1 or SCSI 2 and what devices are compatible.  All I've ever seen on
> these systems were RZ24's, 25's and 26's.

If it's a 50-pin "Centronics" socket then, yes. The manual says you
_should_ have an H8574-A terminator, but in my experience, it's
possible to live without it. I can send you one if necessary. The
manual doesn't say which SCSI, but from the age of the machine, it's
probably SCSI-1.

If you have a 36-pin Centronics socket, it's for an H3104-00 8-way
serial port "harmonica" expander cable. Wiring _was_ at
http://vax.sevensages.org/hw/cables.htm but that didn't respond when I
just tried it.
 
> > Also I've found strange male DB-25 (?) connector near RESET button. What
> > is it? Is it video output or what?
> 
> Combination video/keyboard plug.

Not if it's a _MicroVax_ where this is RS-232 port 2. Ports 0, 1, and
3 are MMJ connectors on its left.

>  You'll need a special cable. Monitor needs to
> be fixed frequency, sync on green.  PeeCee style monitors will not work.

I believe _some_ PC monitors have a sync-on-green option.

