Andy,

1. Every reference I found on the web for DS-RZ1DB-VW states "Ultra SCSI Wide".
The word differential is missing, hence single-ended.

2. I'M CONFUSED!  You stated "All the hard drives work, as they have been tested
in a Compaq Storageworks external cabinent which is not differential."  How on
earth could they work if they were differential and the cabinet is not?

3. Take apart an SBB and write down the drive manufacturer and model of the
drive.  Go to the manufacturer's web site and find the specs for the model of
drive.

4. As far as I know, DEC was smart enough not to fall into the differential SCSI
trap for disk drives accessed at long cabling distances.  The whole point of
these external cabinets was to use more plentiful and cheaper (at the time)
single-ended SCSI drives in large quantities, and use
differential-to-single-ended converters to handle signals over long distances.
This is in contrast to Sun who went whole hog into differential SCSI for its
later server storage, and other old-time mainframers who did the same.  DEC
learned well from its experience with its modified and bastardized DSSI, just
enough different from SCSI to be, well, different.

5. The Compaq web site describes AlphaServer SCSI host adapters for these drives
as single-ended:

http://www.compaq.com/alphaserver/options/as300/as300_15_3_options_sorted.html#OptListHead

But by all means, verify the capabilities of the model(s) of drives inside the
SBBs, to be absolutely sure !  

... Ben Myers

