I just picked up a VAX 4000/200 with a Q-Bus card which I do not
recognize, and I was hoping that someone here would know what it is.

It is a dual-height card, made by TD Systems, Inc. in Lowell, Mass.
and the silkscreen printing on the board identifies it as a "TDL-12,
Assy. 210-00028 (c) 1985"

The paper label on top of a Z80 processor says: "TDL-12 #4101",
and the paper label on top of a 27C128 ROM says "TDL-12 KOM 1.70".
A 20-pin socketed chip at position U13 has a paper label that
simply says "KOM".   A jumper next to that chip appears to control
the Q-Bus address: in the "A" position it responds to 765300-765316,
and in the "B" position it responds to 765400-765416.   In both
cases the first word contains 0081(hex) and the rest are all zeros.

A 50-pin ribbon cable leads to a "Centronics" style connector on
the "skunk box" bulkhead, next to a "KOM, Inc." label.

A photo of the board is at http://www.nelsonusa.com/alan/tdl-12a.jpg

Does anyone recognize it?

Thanks,
Alan

