RCM, Remote Console Management.

This is a non-standard port best forgotten that it exists. The port required
an external battery backed up power source as well as a external battery
backed up modem. This port could be used to control the system, in fact
shutting it down and turning it off or on, re-booting and many other things
like running some low level diags. On many systems that we shipped, the
jumpers were set to disable this port and usually in the console firmware we
turned the thing off as well. Windows NT never liked these ports at all and
to avoid problems with Windows NT these were only ever configured on if they
were actually going to be used with OpenVMS or Tru64 UNIX. That's the
manufacturing story from Kanata and I'm sticking to it, failing memory or
not. The other two serial ports are just like PC ports and are controlled by
the ECU. So, even though the Alpha may not have an EISA bus, the basic
peripherals of the machine, floppy, com1, com2, parallel port, are in fact
controlled by settings in the ECU. If the Alpha has an on-board video, then
that may also have ECU settings, in addition to or instead of on-board
jumpers.

rtt




