It's not worth to buy an Alpha in the US. They are not much more
expensive in Germany.
 
> What AXP boards do exist? Are they AT or ATX format, or do I need special
> cases?

There is that old AXPpci33 board. It's AT, but don't buy it. It has
an 21066. Really slow.
The most common boards are the 164's. These are all 21164 Alphas.
The boards are ATX. Some older ones still is PS2 simms. Newer
boards use standard ECC SD-RAM. Personally I would recommend the 164LX.
The 164UX is similar, has onboard SCSI and Ethernet, but can't run
VMS or Tru64, because there is no SRM firmware. The 164SX is almost
the same as the LX, but it only has an 21164PC. That's a somewhat
crippled Alpha with less cache than the real chip. That makes it
a bit slower for int, but quiet a bit for fp tasks. The 164LX has
a real 21164, 2MB cache and there is SRM for this board.
You should use google to get an impression what PCI cards are
supported by VMS or Tru64.

