No, it would not, because you would not be using Microsoft's intellectual
property (which you have no right to use without having obtained it from
them in some manner they agree to) to accomplish whatever you wanted to.

Presumably you have some reason to prefer to use Win2K instead of *BSD or
Linux.  If that's the case, Win2K is conferring some benefit, and Microsoft,
being its owner, has the right to profit from it (and for that matter
Microsoft has the right to control its intellectual property even if it
confers no benefit).  If you choose to use some other system instead, that's
of course your right - but if you want to run Win2K, you need to under the
rules its owner has the right to set for doing so.

You really should go back and re-read Peter's previous response:  it covered
both the legal and ethical aspects of this situation very well.  Given
Microsoft's general behavior, I would certainly have no compunction
whatsoever in doing exactly what you propose doing if I happened to have an
Alpha sitting around and wanted to run Win2K on it for purely personal use,
but I wouldn't try to assert that doing so was legal or ethical:  I'd admit
that I was choosing to operate on or somewhat beyond the fringes of legality
and ethics because that's what Microsoft itself does and responding in kind
seems appropriate to me.

