Ah, Grasshopper.  Your problem is in failing to understand what you see and
hear.  And your 'logic' above is not only faulty but irrelevant, since if
people didn't *believe* that their platform was worth worrying about
(regardless of what you may think of its worth) they'd just immediately move
to another without bothering to wave good-bye let alone discuss things for
over a year.

As for the observations and discussions that you seem to confuse with
'complaints', most are not so much about anyone getting screwed (at least
not immediately) as about how cHumPaq screwed *up* and what, if anything,
should be done about it.

In killing Alpha cHumPaq acted stupidly, since Alpha had major potential for
generating profit.  Question:  what do you do when your vendor is
demonstrably incompetent?

In killing Alpha cHumPaq acted unethically, since it had made repeated,
solemn, public, unequivocal commitments to Alpha's future through EV8, EV9,
and EV10 (and more nebulous commitments to a 25-year lifetime) - right up
until the date it broke them without any consultation with, or hint of
apology to, those to whom it had made those commitments, nor any compelling
reason that might excuse such behavior.  Question:  what do you do when your
vendor is demonstrably unethical?

In explaining why it killed Alpha cHumPaq lied about Alpha's potential
future performance compared with the Itanic alternative, about where this
misinformation came from, and about Alpha's current (let alone potential)
profitability.  Question:  what do you do when your vendor demonstrably (and
repeatedly) lies to you?

Since killing Alpha cHumPaq has done nothing to attempt to compensate for
the decidedly chilling effect that being available only on a declared-dying
platform (with a future dependent on a yet-to-be-accepted platform) has had
on perceived VMS viability, both in the existing customer base and in the
market at large.  Question:  what do you do when your vendor exhibits zero
interest in (let alone any real 'commitment' to) the platform you depend
upon?

For some people, the answer is that you just accept it, hope for the best,
and defend your vendor (because it's the only one you've got, regardless of
how incompetent and sleazy it may be).  For others the answer is that you
accept it and hope for the best (because you don't see any alternative) but
stop short of becoming an active accessory to such behavior.  Yet others
don't accept the behavior but don't get actively involved either, because it
may be professionally awkward (they're the people from whom I get "me, too"
emails that include a request not to quote them - and I appreciate that
support and recognize why they're reticent to go public).  A fourth group
just get disgusted and leave, without wasting their time and energy in
extensive comment about a vendor they will no longer be worrying about.  And
finally there are those who don't accept the behavior and decide to try to
do something to fix the problems that caused it, because they fear that if
they don't VMS will very likely go the way of Alpha, and with equally little
warning - or simply because they feel that corporations that act like this
should be horse-whipped until the lesson sticks, for the public good.

The tension here is between the first group (plus those in more active
collusion with cHumPaq) and the last group, but is obviously of interest to
at least some people in most of the other groups as well, even if not to
you.

- bill





